# Multiple Sequence Alignment (MSA)

## Overview

Multiple Sequence Alignment (MSA) is a bioinformatics technique used to align three or more biological sequences to identify conserved
regions, sequence variations, and evolutionary relationships. In this project, Clustal Omega was used to perform the alignment of
homologous protein sequences obtained from BLAST analysis.

## Objective

The objective of this analysis was to identify conserved amino acid residues, compare sequence similarities and differences among
homologous proteins, and generate an aligned dataset for phylogenetic analysis.

## Methodology

Protein sequences retrieved from BLAST were collected in FASTA format and submitted to Clustal Omega for multiple sequence alignment.
The aligned sequences were analyzed to identify conserved regions and sequence variations. The resulting alignment was then used as the
input for phylogenetic tree construction.

## Input

* Homologous protein sequences in FASTA format.
* Clustal Omega alignment tool.

## Output

* Multiple sequence alignment (.aln).
* Aligned FASTA file.
* Conserved and variable sequence regions.
* Guide tree generated during alignment (if applicable).

## Key Features

* Aligns multiple protein sequences simultaneously.
* Identifies conserved amino acid residues.
* Detects sequence variations and gaps.
* Produces alignment files for downstream analyses.

## Applications

* Conserved region identification.
* Comparative sequence analysis.
* Protein family studies.
* Preparation for phylogenetic tree construction.

## Files Included

* Input protein sequences (FASTA).
* Multiple sequence alignment file (.aln).
* Aligned FASTA file.
* Guide tree (if generated).

## Conclusion

Multiple sequence alignment successfully aligned the homologous protein sequences and identified conserved and variable regions among 
them. The alignment provided valuable insights into sequence conservation and served as the foundation for subsequent phylogenetic
analysis.

